            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Latin for to ease, then the Latin for tame."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: grātus, grāta, grātum",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "grātus, grāta, grātum — \"welcome, pleasing; grateful\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Epistula tua mihi grāta est. Your letter is welcome to me."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Two sides: pleasing to someone (with the dative), and grateful for something. Grātiās agō, already known, belongs here. English grateful."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "grātus is inherited and traced to a Proto-Indo-European root meaning \"to praise, welcome\" (EtymDB); Lewis & Short compares Greek charis. English grateful and gratitude come from its family."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
